1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned registry of open mathematical conjectures, their status, the partial results, and the relations between them. A conjecture is a mathematical statement that is believed to be true but has not been proved or disproved: the Riemann hypothesis, the Collatz conjecture, Goldbach's conjecture, the twin prime conjecture, P versus NP, the Hodge conjecture, and many others. The Mathematical Conjecture Registry records each conjecture with its statement, its field, its status, the partial results, the implications, the relations to other conjectures, and its provenance.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for mathematicians, mathematics students, researchers, and anyone who wants to know the state of an open problem.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of mathematical conjectures from users.

Store each conjecture with its statement, its field, its status, and its provenance.

Record the statement: the formal statement and the informal description.

Record the field and subfield: number theory, algebra, geometry, topology, analysis, logic, combinatorics, probability, or other.

Record the status: open, proved, disproved, partially_resolved, or undecided.

Record the partial results: what has been proved toward the conjecture.

Record the implications: what would follow if the conjecture were proved or disproved.

Record the relations to other conjectures: which imply, are implied by, generalize, specialize, or are equivalent to others.

Record the history: who stated the conjecture, when, and where.

Record the prize or recognition associated with the conjecture, if any.

Record the known counterexamples or partial counterexamples.

Record the computational checks: what has been verified computationally and up to what bound.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by conjecture ID, name, field, status, author, year, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the statement, the informal description, the partial results, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by field, by status, by author, by year, by prize.

Support exporting a conjecture as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a conjecture from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a conjecture to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the conjecture is described in a deposited work.

Support linking a conjecture to a Numerical Verification Tool result if the conjecture's computational checks have been verified.

Support the conjecture graph: a visualisation of the conjectures as nodes and the relations as edges.

Support the implication map: a visualisation of what follows from each conjecture.

Support the conjecture timeline: a visualisation of the conjectures and their partial results over time.

Support the field map: a visualisation of the conjectures by field and subfield.

Support the status view: a visualisation of the conjectures by status.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical conjecture in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 conjectures.

Versioned: the registry records the STAG schema version, the mathematical conjecture schema version, and the registry version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

